Bluetooth: preserve central CIS SDU limits on establishment

Local experimental fix, rebased onto Linux 7.3.1 (first written against 6.18.57).

The central supplies Max_SDU_C_To_P and Max_SDU_P_To_C through LE Set CIG
Parameters, using conn->iso_qos. LE CIS Established instead reports Max_PDU
in c_mtu/p_mtu. Replacing the configured SDU limits with these values rejects
valid SDUs spanning more than one radio PDU in iso_send_frame.

On z14's MT7922, 248 kb/s stereo LC3 negotiates a 310-byte SDU, while the
controller reports a 155-byte Max_PDU with BN=2. Every 310-byte send then
fails with EMSGSIZE, despite successful CIS establishment.

Retain the central's configured SDU sizes for enabled directions. Preserve
the existing zero-BN behavior and leave the peripheral path unchanged.
No packet-size bypass or assumed PDU-to-SDU multiplier is required.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -7039,8 +7039,14 @@
 		qos->ucast.in.latency =
 			DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST(get_unaligned_le24(ev->p_latency),
 					  1000);
-		qos->ucast.out.sdu = ev->c_bn ? le16_to_cpu(ev->c_mtu) : 0;
-		qos->ucast.in.sdu = ev->p_bn ? le16_to_cpu(ev->p_mtu) : 0;
+		/* The central configured Max_SDU in LE Set CIG Parameters.
+		 * c_mtu/p_mtu report Max_PDU, which can be smaller than an
+		 * SDU. Keep the configured limits for enabled directions.
+		 */
+		if (!ev->c_bn)
+			qos->ucast.out.sdu = 0;
+		if (!ev->p_bn)
+			qos->ucast.in.sdu = 0;
 		qos->ucast.out.phys = le_phy_qos(ev->c_phy);
 		qos->ucast.in.phys = le_phy_qos(ev->p_phy);
 		break;
